1. Introduction
Heat Exchangers (HXs) are crucial components for thermal management. Exchanging heat between two fluids is the primary use of HXs, which creates applications in a broad range of engineering systems such as power plants, oil refineries, air conditioning, automotive, oil coolers in aircrafts, high-temperature resistojets, compact electronic cooling, chemical processes, waste heat recovery devices (e.g. recuperator) in furnaces and hydrogen plants, energy storage systems, and energy conversion systems such as refrigeration cycles [1–6]. For all practical purposes, the overall system reliability depends heavily on effective heat transfer [7]. There are distinct types of HXs based on process and operating environment [1] with the primary goal of maximum heat transfer as well as lowest pressure drop, i.e. pumping power consumptions [3]. Shell and tube HXs are the most common type of HXs for liquid-liquid applications. There are some concerns about the size and space limitation for this type of HXs, but innovations are underway [8, 9]. The other prevalent type of HXs is plate-and-frame which are normally applied for two liquids. Various patterns of plate and layout are being used in this type of HXs [4]. In essence, the HXs specific application dictates its final type [10]. There are also various forms of passive heat transfer devices such as heat pipe (HP) and heat sinks (HS). Heat pipes (HP) are two-phase devices that transfer heat at high rate based on thermal conductivity and phase change of the working fluid. There are distinct mechanisms in HPs to help return condensate flow to evaporator, which include gravity, wicking effect, magnetic field, and centrifugal force [11]. Recently, there has been growing interests in redesigning traditional HXs, which dates back to decades. Besides, there is an increasing trend in developing small-size HXs, particularly for compact electronic devises [7]. In this way, additive manufacturing (AM) technologies has been considered as an effective prescription to remove the limits of traditional manufacturing techniques and opens a new chapter in HXs design and development. AM refers to a class of technologies based on layer-by-layer fabrication using 3D model data [12]. There are several examples of novel HX design in the literature that sound very challenging, if not impossible, to be fabricated through traditional manufacturing processes. AM has created a world of opportunity to redesign HXs in pioneering forms, shapes, and sizes. In other words, AM is able to facilitate the need for higher surface area to volume ratio in heat transfer devices. Moreover, AM is a new opening for devices such as microchannel HXs, which could not find broad applications due to high cost of conventional manufacturing methods. There are many comprehensive studies in the literature explaining various AM technologies. For the sake of conciseness, this study will not provide the details for various AM technologies and readers are referred to Ref. [13, 14]. The main metal technologies considered in this study are powder bed fusion (PBF) and powder feed AM [15]. Generally, powder additive manufacturing methods are divided into two categories based on powder delivery system: powder


3
feed and powder bed. In powder bed AM category, powder was delivered first into one layer of bed spread either by roller, scraper, or blade. Then the powder was solidified into desired shape by heat source from laser, electron beam, UV source, or other electromagnetic fields. The powder bed AM fabrication methods include the following commonly used printing techniques: Direct metal laser sintering (DMLS), Electron beam melting (EBM), Selective heat sintering (SHS), Selective laser melting (SLM) and Selective laser sintering (SLS). The high-power electron beam would lead to parts with better mechanical strength compared to other metal AM technologies. On the contrast, in powder feed AM category the powder is delivered and solidified simultaneously layer by layer. Powder feed method is faster and more cost efficient. Powder bed method is generally more precise with higher resolution. The most well-known powder feed AM technique is direct energy deposition (DED) where metal feedstock will be deposited directly onto a build tray or an existing part by an energy source, building up free form three-dimensional parts [12]. The material is deposited as continuous flow of powders or by means of wire feedstock. Application of DED in heat exchanger and related functional components has been expanded into two directions: one is on large scale and fast rate with the goal to decrease cost and increase efficiency; the other one is on expand design and materials freedom, increase resolution and decrease roughness. Both two directions are critical to success fabrication of lighter, cheaper and easier made rocket engine for NASA [16–19]. There is another form of powder feed AM process known as cold spray, which applies kinetic energy to establish solid-state bonds. Cold-gas dynamic spraying, or simply cold spraying (CS), is a solid-state powder deposition technology in which powder consolidation is made by high velocity impact of accelerated particles on a build surface [20]. This fabrication technique will be elaborated in Section 3.2. In addition, although laminated object manufacturing (LOM) found some use for HXs manufacturing, this AM technique is not as mature as SLM and DED in providing accurate and high-quality parts. This review study intends to highlight the challenges and opportunities in design and manufacturing of HXs. Section 2 is dedicated to the design opportunities and challenges in fabricating HXs using AM technologies. Section 3 evaluates opportunities and challenges offered by AM from manufacturing point of view. In section 4, the significant insights in experimental characterization of additively manufactured HXs are extensively considered. Section 5 provide the conclusions and the future directions.
2. HX Design using AM
There is always a definitive need for compact and efficient HXs. This implies a demand for optimal heat transfer in a smaller space with less material, i.e. higher heat transfer and reduced mass to volume ratio [5]. Heat transfer enhancement for single-phase devices comes normally with an increase in pressure drop between the channel inlet and outlet [5], especially in micro-channels [1]. In essence, low pressure drops ensure low pumping power requirements [7]. Thus, design goals for HXs can be summarized as maximizing the ratio of heat transfer to pressure drop [21]. In addition, for two-phase flow devices, understanding the flow pattern is critical. Several studies considered two-phase flow in additively made heat pipes and porous lattice structures [22–26].


