2.1 Design opportunities
Cost-effective fabrication of complex and compact designs through AM has led many researchers to embrace this significant opportunity and explore new HX designs such as bioinspired wavy microchannels [21], miniature water-cooled HX [27], innovative manifold microchannel [28, 29], topology-optimized HX [7], tree-like distributor for a multi-channel HXreactor [30], innovative Pitot HS [31], airfoil pin fins [32], internally finned tubes [33], finless tube bundle with novel shapes [34], and webbed-Tube HX [35] to name a few. Figure 1 shows some examples of novel HX design fabricated via AM. It is important to note that some of the new HX designs have not been tested in actual applications. For instance, Cárdenas et al. [36] used hexagonal mesh for the cross-sectional area for an air-to-air HX. Although it was not proved experimentally, the authors stated the new design may result in significant volume reduction per unit of energy transfer [36]. Saltzman et al. [1] presented a new design of aircraft oil cooler which required major geometry changes for manufacturability. A novel HS design presented in [37] and fabricated via powder bed fusion, could reduce the volume and weight by 98% and 94%, respectively. Gerstler and Erno [38] came up with a new design of multi-furcating HXs for fuelcooled oil cooler applications using four different materials and SLM technology. While they achieved 66% lower weight and 50% lower volume, the performance of additive HXs was found to be equivalent to a conventional design [38]. Bernardin et al. [39] designed a compact twisted tube shell HX fabricated via SLM. The performance analysis was merely done numerically. Comparing to the traditional round tube HXs, this type of swirl design is a better design approach because it generates a secondary flow within the axial flow and leads to reduction of the boundary layer.
