Microchannel HXs seem promising because of their high heat transfer rates. In effect, novel designs of manifold-microchannel HXs reduce the microchannel flow length, and therefore, resolve the issue of high pressure drop which is intrinsic to narrow flow channels [3]. In an attempt to develop more affordable dry cooling for power plants, Arie et al. [41] studied three air-water manifold-microchannel HXs made by aluminum alloy (AlSi10Mg), stainless steel (SS17-4), and titanium alloy (Ti64). The water side of HX comprised rectangular channels in a crossflow configuration. The authors considered the temperature difference between the inlet water and the inlet air at the manifold-microchannels (∆TT). They showed Ti64 prototype could improve gravimetric heat transfer density by 25%-30% compared to wavy fins and plain plate fins. In this respect, the gravimetric heat transfer can be defined as QQ/mm∆TT, where m is the heat exchanger mass and Q is heat flow rate. Similar to HXs, chemical reactors are also receiving the benefits of AM-enabled designs. For example, multi-furcating configuration was explored by Gerstler and Erno [38]. The complex multifurcated design decreased weight 66% and shrank the volume more than 50% when keeping the same heat transfer efficiency and pressure drop as the traditional design. More elaborate architectures, such as mixing structures, residence time channels, separation units, and interfaces for in-line analysis, have been incorporated into within microreactors and millireactors. One exemplary work has been performed by research group of Gutmann et al. [42], in which a reactor for continuous difluoromethylations using fluoroform was designed and additively manufactured using stainless steel 316L (see Figure 2). They claim their 3D printed device is the first metal reactor in the world for synthesis. Shortcut holes were designed at every turn for easy removal of powder. After the channels were cleared and the reactor was cleaned with ultrasound, the holes were closed by laser beam welding and 1/16′′ stainless steel tubes were attached onto the inlets and outlets. The tubes were finally equipped with standard 1/16′′ fittings to allow simple integration with peripheral flow equipment.
