2.2 Important design features
This section highlights important HXs design features which can be fabricated using AM technologies but relatively challenging to be fabricated through conventional methods. The new or enhanced design features - such as microgrooves, louver features, and vortex generators - are expected to assist in increasing the surface to volume ratio as well as reducing the volume to weight ratio to some extent. In this section, certain significant features enabled by AM with potential for performance enhancement are considered. In this respect, vortex generators has been mentioned by a handful of researchers as an effective design tool for enhancing heat transfer without severe effect on air-side pressure drop [1]. Vortex generators are known as a type of fin which disturb boundary layer flows [5]. Aris et al. [43] applied SLM technology to fabricate active delta wing vortex generators made from shape memory alloy materials for microelectronics applications. In their experimental investigation, the authors in [43] demonstrated heat transfer enhancement of up to 90% and 7% pressure loss by the single wing vortex generators mounted on a heated rectangular channel surface. In addition, internal rifling was used by Tiwari et al. [3] to improve heat transfer in a tubular heat exchanger. Internal rifling would disturb the temperature gradients near the tube surface. Moreover, wavy channels have been a subject of study in order to enhance heat transfer and reduce the size and pressure drop in [21], [44], and [45]. One can expect higher heat transfer in wavy channels compared to straight channels. Kirsch and Thole demonstrated that longer wavelength channels provide higher heat transfer than short wavelength channels. High pressure drop was reported in short wavelength channels [45]. The most noteworthy trend in redesigning HXs is the elimination of conventional fins. Although fin arrays can provide compact design and reduction in thermal resistance, one should expect increase in viscous resistance and higher manufacturing costs. Traditionally, fins are stamped and then brazed to the base plate. In high pressure HXs, brazing is replaced by diffusion welding. Continuous fin arrays can be divided into two major classes: plate or wavy. It is evident that the heat transfer performance depends heavily on fin size, number of fin layers, and type of fins. Nevertheless, reinforced by AM technologies, there is a tendency to transform traditional continuous fin to discrete pin fin arrays. The notable properties of pin-fin arrays are as follows: high volumetric heat transfer [46, 47], augmented turbulent mixing [4], potential increase in pressure drop [48], and durability concerns due to high mechanical and thermal stresses. Heat transfer in pin-fin arrays is a function of pin geometries such as pin size and shape, fin array parameters such as pin spacing, roughness level, and shroud clearance [49]. Interestingly, Kirsch and Thole [10] showed similar heat transfer performance for two pin-fin arrays in which one array has six times more pins. Similar results were reported by Alsulamin et al. for manifoldmicrochannel heat exchanger [50]. In essence, understanding tradeoff between design complexity and performance would be very helpful for weight reduction as well as manufacturing cycle time. In addition, based on the thermo-hydraulic performance comparison presented in [5], it seems additively fabricated pin fin arrays provide higher friction factor augmentation compared to that of wavy microchannels. Considering the pin geometries, Wong et al. concluded that elliptical fins, made via SLM, have better heat transfer per unit pressure drop compared to rectangular and cylindrical pin [51]. They also examined lattice shaped fins and indicated no promising heat transfer improvement despite larger heat transfer surface area [51]. In addition, it is important to point out that higher


