density. The authors concluded that fin density and clavate are important contributing factor in the droplet dynamics [55]. The SLM technology has been considered by researchers to design freeform HPs with innovative shape, optimized wick structure, and compact size to expose novel applications. Ameli et al. [23] applied SLM to produce sintered wick structures for their aluminum based heat pipe. The wick structures in that work are different in thickness, porosity, permeability and pore sizes [23]. Esarte et al. [22] came up with a novel loop heat pipe (LHP) for cooling purposes. The performance of LHP devices depend on the design of the evaporator and primary wick. Using SLM, the researchers in [22] optimized the wick performance by embedding internal passages into the primary wick and scaffold porosity of 17%. A couple of research groups have worked on pulsating heat pipe (PHP) which requires no capillary structures. Among them, Thompson et al. [56] introduced a novel design of compact flat-plate PHP. They employed the concept of “vent holes” into their HP in order to de-powder the internal long channels layer-by-layer. In a similar work, Ibrahim et al. [40] tested a multi-layered PHP with several working fluid and different operating orientations. In addition, Jafari et al. [57] highlighted the significance of interstitial fluid on thermal conductivity based on the experimental results on SS316L porous HPs. The SLM technology also offers the opportunity of fabricating innovative porous structures to suit various applications [23, 58]. A solid matrix with an interconnected void is known as porous media [59]. The possibility of fabricating various porous cell topologies makes this area even more attractive for designers particularly for improving area to volume ratio (which enhance boiling heat transfer) and capillary scale two-phase heat transfer equipment. Nevertheless, for small capillaries, it is challenging to establish a practical trade-off of some conflicting factors such as permeability and thermal conductivity. Beside the design challenges, there are some manufacturing concerns including accurate scanning speed, heat source power, build orientation, powder adhesion, and design of the unit cell [23, 58, 60, 61]. In other words, both process parameters and design parameters need to be optimized to achieve a precise porous structure. This opens a new research direction in this area. Hence, further investigations are necessary to reveal the potentials of porous media for HX applications and characterize the thermal-fluid behavior. Ho et al. [24] investigated heat transfer in a SLM-fabricated saturated pool boiling which was redesigned with micro-cavity and micro-fin surfaces features. The experimental comparison with commercially available plain Al-6061 surface showed the positive effects of AM-enabled features on enhancing heat transfer performances. In similar studies [25, 26, 62] , the researchers confirmed the significant effects of SLM-fabricated porous structures on heat transfer coefficients, hydrodynamic choking, and critical heat flux. In [26], two-phase study of porous metallic structures revealed higher pressure drop at adiabatic condition.
2.3 Geometrical optimization
There can be no doubt that AM has given a boost to topology optimization (TO). Unlike traditional parametric design approaches, TO is a numerical top-down approach that generate efficient geometry under a prescribed objective and constraints [7]. In traditional practices, optimization of heat transfer devises is performed by manipulating geometric parameters in commercial computational fluid dynamics (CFD) software packages. Reducing design cycle time and demanding less computational power are the potential benefits of TO. Techniques of TO can


9
be classified into three branches; (i) density-based method, (ii) Evolutionary Structural Optimization (ESO) methods, and (iii) Level-Set based Method (LSM) [63]. AM-based TO faces numerous challenges. In practice, TO outcomes include organic freeform surface with a large number of long overhang features, which creates the need of support structures. Research is currently underway to overcome these challenges [64]. For instance, Gaynor and Guest proposed a TO framework using overhang constraint to minimize the need for support materials [65]. Recently, more attention has been given to lattice structure topology optimization (LSTO) for its proven ability to maintain the original design and the limited need for support structures [66]. Also within this context, Cheng et al [63] applied LSTO with implicit boundary conditions and designed a cooling channel with enhanced performance. Koga et al. [67] worked on topology-optimized cooling channels with application to small scale heat sinks used in compact electronic components. The authors recognized the practicality of TO methods to solve multi-objective and multi-physics problems, i.e. maximizing heat dissipation combined with minimizing pressure drop. Das and Sutradhar [68] worked on generating functionally graded porous structure assisted by multi-physics topology optimization for heat-dissipating structures. Bacellar et al. [34] presented shape optimization using automated computational fluid dynamics simulations for a small HX with finless tubes. Their optimized design outperformed the baseline microchannel HX and showed over 50% potential size reduction. The authors in [34] believe all design variables (not only tube shape) should be taken into account to achieve better performance. Recent studies in this field have focused on design optimization for two-fluid heat exchanger. This class of topology optimization involves two-fluid and one-solid distribution. In this regard, Tawk et al. [69] extended the density-based approach in twodimensional examples for bi-fluid applications with the objectives of minimizing the pressure drop and maximizing the heat transfer. Kobayashi et al. [70] stretched the two-dimensional two-fluid studies into three dimensions under the constraint of a fixed pressure loss. A detailed discussion on topology optimization is out of the scope of the current study. The interested readers are referred to [64, 71] and the recent review study on fluid-based topology optimization [72].
3. HX fabrication using AM
This section intends to highlight the remarkable opportunities offered by various AM technologies to fabricate efficient heat transfer devices. It is important to point out there are many non-additive manufacturing methods to fabricate HXs, such as chemical etching and diffusion bonding. Some of these methods are versatile in using different materials and fabricate with high accuracy. However, the main objective of this section is to highlight the main advantages of AM over traditional manufacturing particularly for HX applications. AM technologies in general and SLM in particular have a great potential to fabricate practical HXs. There are several important considerations in this respect including cost (vs quantity) [73], complexity [50, 74], and microstructural properties [75]. In essence, the prevalence of metal AM technologies is contingent on overcoming a number of technical challenges such as lack of standards, limited material options, setting suitable process parameters, difficulties in characterizing thermophysical properties, and acquiring the desired surface texture. This section starts with material-related challenges.


