3.1 AM Materials
HXs have been made using all classes of materials, i.e. metal, plastic, ceramic, and composites. In material selection for AM, one needs to deliberate mechanical, chemical and thermal properties [4]. However, for heat transfer devices, thermal conductivity and durability are the key factors in the material selection process [1]. It should be emphasized that applicable materials for metal AM applications are growing, albeit rather slowly. In essence, high temperature materials, such as ferritic steels, Ni-based alloys and ceramics, are more favorable for certain HX application [4]. This section starts with metallic materials which is classified to ferrous, nonferrous, and superalloys. Table 1 shows the popular material for additively manufactured HXs.
3.1.1 Metals
Ferrous alloys (e.g. SS347, SS316L, etc.) are low cost materials with an extensive application in heat transfer devices. In particular, ferrous alloys are considered in HX designs with substantial components thermal expansion. On the other hand, nonferrous alloys might be better candidates for additively manufactured HXs applications. For instance, Aluminum alloys are favorable in SLM applications because of significant properties such as high strength-to-weight ratio and high thermal diffusivity. Wong et al. [76] showed that AL6061 can outperform SS 316L in air-cooling HSs fabricated with various fin geometries through SLM. Ho et al. [32] used AlSi10Mg powder to fabricate a novel airfoil HS with application to integrated circuit boards. It should be noted that AlSi10Mg is an appropriate material for electronic cooling because of relatively high thermal conductivity and light weight. In addition, because of corrosion-resistant properties of magnesium, AlSi10Mg is more preferable than other aluminum alloys such as AlSi12.
Copper has excellent thermal conductivity, and therefore, it is in demand for HXs applications and internal cooling channels. However, metal AM technologies are not mature enough to fabricate copper AM parts. This is mainly due to low laser radiation absorption and high thermal conductivity which creates the need for higher energy input [77]. In essence, research is underway to enable SLM and laser-based-DED systems to fabricate reflective metal such as copper or aluminum. For example, Zhang et al. [78] successfully fabricated components of Cu-15Ni-8Sn alloy with near full density of 99.4%. This high-performance and corrosion-resistant copper alloy sounds suitable for HXs applications. Popovich et al. [77] investigated the SLM fabrication of CuCr-Zr-Ti alloy and achieved high density of high density of 97.9%. In this alloy, Zr an Ti are added to improve hot ductility. To improve the mechanical properties, the authors in [77] suggested further process parameters optimization to reduce residual porosity. Moriya et al. [79] used the similar alloy, i.e. Cu-Cr-Zr, in a SLM fabrication system and successfully prototyped a combustion chamber inner liner for very high-temperature applications. Sing et al. [80] reported effective AM methods in fabricating Cu alloys, Cu composite, and embedded copper structures. Such AM methods would provide the essential platform for more novel designs of multi-metal HXs, particularly in the field of electronics/electronic cooling. In essence, multi-material HXs is a great opportunity offered by AM in which smooth transition between two materials would remove joining and welding [19, 81–83]. Bimetallic or multi-alloy configuration for channel wall nozzles has been preliminarily investigated in [19]. Hot wall and inner liner were C-18150 copper alloy


