Superalloys are an important group of heat-resistant materials in fabricating HX particularly for aerospace applications. Although superalloys normally come with high cost and low thermal conductivity, they are valued for elevated temperature applications as they can retain certain properties. In addition, despite their high toughness, they may suffer from crack formation during welding [4]. Among superalloys, titanium alloys have gained dramatically increasing attention from AM community because of cost advantages with respect to machining and long lead times [13, 14]. Research groups particularly paid attention to Ti-6Al-4V for all metal manufacturing processes [99, 100]. This alloy has been investigated for AM-fabricated HXs [38] and heat pipes [40, 56]. Increase in heat transfer was reported in [87] for air-water manifold microchannel HX made by Ti-6Al-4V. Nickel-based super alloys are also suitable materials for various HXs applications. For high temperature applications, Inconel 718 provides high strength and excellent creep behavior. It should be pointed out that AM technologies are seriously being considered for Inconel 718 and other nickel-based superalloys mainly because of the potential for reduced production steps and enhanced geometrical complexity [82]. Made by Inconel 718 through SLM, Zhang et al. [88] demonstrated a light-weight gas-to-gas manifold microchannel HX with 25% higher heat transfer density at 600°C. Seiya and Zhang [85] pointed out the importance of post-print material properties for couple of materials including Inconel 718. Seiya and Zhang [85] also highlighted the economic viability in using metal AM technologies. AM technologies, particularly laser-based DED, have created an unprecedented opportunity for various types of functionally graded components. This offers a huge potential for designers to produce variable-property and cost-effective HXs by density control, dimensionality control, or mixing various materials [101]. Although the technologies related to functionally graded AM is still immature, it will emerge as a feasible performance modeling approach in the


