near future. In actual practice, the literature suggests several examples of promising studies. For instance, Onuike et al. [102] investigated a functionally graded material (FGM) with transition from Inconel 718 to a copper alloy (GRCop-84). This FGM shows 250% increase in diffusivity and 300% increase in conductivity compared to pure Inconel 718 alloy [102]. A density-controlled functionally graded Inconel 718 was produced via SLM by Popovich et al [103]. This investigation reveals a substantial improvement in thermomechanical life of the functionally graded Inconel 718 after heat treatment [103].
3.1.2 Ceramics
In the past two decades, ceramic materials have gained increasing attention to be used for HXs mainly in high temperature applications [4]. Ceramic HXs for high pressure application need to use monolithic bonds, i.e. permanent bonds. Furthermore, non-monolithic bonds provide the chance to be de-bounded to fix or replace the damaged parts. AM technologies have faced numerous challenges for processing ceramic materials. In particular, direct deposition techniques impose some limitations to fabricate ceramics. The challenges include but are not limited to feedstock and sintering [104]. In this context, a pioneer study by Alm et al. [105], applied stereolithography (SLA) to fabricate the injection mold and produced a ceramic plate-and-frame HX. Scheithauer et al. [106] introduced lithography-based ceramic manufacturing as an alternative of SLA with advantages such as better component properties and higher resolution. Shulman and Ross [107] prototyped a compact ceramic HX using laminated object manufacturing (LOM). In this process, ceramic green tapes were laser cut and then precisely stacked to build a HX with internal features. They applied zirconia-toughened mullite (ZTM) which offers several benefits for high temperature HX including ease of densification and relatively low cost. The prototype could withstand high temperature operating conditions over 700°C. This study reported damaging defects for larger dimensions. Pelanconi et al. [108] developed tubular heat exchangers with alumina and lattice structure using three different cellular architectures. The authors applied SLA technique to fabricate the lattices and achieved 160% to 280% optimized heat transfer between cold flowing air and the ceramic tube with close agreement between CFD and experimental results [108]. Despite numerous challenges, it is expected that powder-based AM will become the dominant technology to fabricate ceramics in near future [104]. This could be very beneficial for HX community.
3.1.3. Polymers
Polymers by far are the most used AM material. Favorable properties such as low cost, relatively low weight, anti-fouling and anticorrosion has distinguished the application of polymers. However, low thermal conductivity is the major concern for widespread application of polymers in HX systems. To address this issue, Cevallos [35] introduced thermally enhanced polymers. Arie et al. [41] fabricated an air-to-water polymer heat exchanger by polyethylene sheets. Experimental tests in [41] demonstrated that polymer HX could provide equal or superior performance for the same pressure drop compared to plain plate fin surfaces. They measured an overall heat transfer coefficient of 35-120 W/m2K for an air-side flow rate of 3-24 ll/s and a water-side flow rate of 12.5 m ll/s. This research group has also worked on a cross media approach and developed a polymer composite HX with over 200% improvement in heat flow rate over mass [109]. Other than polymer


