heat exchangers, composite heat exchangers additively manufactured from a polymer and a high conductive metal fibers is also reported in literature [110]. A comprehensive review of polymeric HXs are presented in [111, 112]. Here, the challenges and opportunities are highlighted for the sake of comparison.
3.2 Cold Spray
This section provides more details about CS as a promising technique to additively manufacture HXs. The CS process uses low-temperature high-pressure gas jet to accelerate particles with micrometer scale size. The low processing temperature is helpful to (i) produce oxide-free deposits, (ii) retain the feedstock microstructures and properties, (iii) diminish adverse effect on the underlying build surface, (iv) omit the need for a protected environment [20, 113]. The metallurgical bonding is merely caused by kinetic energy with high strain rate plastic deformation and sporadic powder melting. Therefore, ideal materials for CS applications have low melting point and low mechanical strength (e.g. Al, Zn, Cu). This is a limitation for CS as the majority of high strength materials such as Ti and Ni need sufficient energy for successful bonding. CS has found fabrication and repair applications in AM. It has a great potential to be used for HX applications in order to reduce cost, provide higher production rate, and enable large-scale implementation [86]. To fabricate pin fins array, a mask is applied to selectively shield the build surface [47], as shown in Figure 4. Using this techniques, tapered pin fins such as pyramids or cones can be fabricated [47, 53, 54]. Cormier et al. [96] applied cold spray to produce square base pyramidal pin fin arrays. They concluded that higher fins would increase thermal conductance at low Reynolds numbers, but the pressure loss needs further investigations. The challenges and considerations in clod spray AM are quite similar to other AM technologies and include
• Need for post-machining process and post-treatment methods [113], which was not extensively addressed in the literature.
• A thorough understanding of anisotropic properties and residual stress emanated by high velocity impact. In this respect, Yang et al. [114] experimentally showed the existence of mechanical anisotropy in cold-sprayed copper bulk.
• A systematic investigation of the spraying parameters (e.g. scanning steps and gas parameters) to understand and optimize their influence on the deposit properties.
