3.3 Fabrication opportunities and challenges
AM technologies have many advantages which can be directly applied to HX design and manufacturing. First and foremost, AM has the potential to fabricate highly customized freeform features. It also allows multi-material fabrication and design optimization with minimal added manufacturing costs. Depending on the design and machine capability, AM technologies offer faster design process and higher production rating along with saving in material and energy. Nonetheless, there are many design-related considerations that affect the manufacturability and need to be taken into account. For example, thicker walls are required for low strength materials; the orientation of some features might change due to limited build volume; horizontal channels may face size limit; and fins may experience crack or thermal distortion. Furthermore, the size and topology of certain features would impact other design features in metal AM. For instance, spacing in HX channels is the determining factor for pins’ shapes [10]. The interested readers are referred to the existing design guidelines [115, 116]. This section confers the opportunities and challenges that HX community is facing to fully benefit from metal AM. Surface roughness characterization, particularly for micro geometries, is still a major challenge in metal AM. Surface roughness is comparatively high in various metal AM technologies [53, 117]. In effect, multiple factors contribute to surface finish of AM components [10, 59, 117]. Process parameters such as energy density, layer thickness, and scanning speed, are the main sources of controlling surface roughness [118, 119]. There are complicated non-linear interactions between process parameters in laser sintering [120]. Any process parameter with impact on the partial melting of the powders can affect the surface roughness [59]. To name a few, build direction [121, 122], material [122], size of powder particles [59], arrangement of parts in the buildup chamber and heat accumulation [59], sloping angle [117], types of features (i.e. downward vs. upward-facing) [123], and support for overhanging features are among the factors influencing surface roughness. Rough surface finish was reported for downward facing surfaces due to higher heat accumulation [10]. Accordingly, process parameters such as laser power are more significant in controlling the surface quality of downward facing surfaces and features in open channel space [123]. Mumtaz and Hopkinson investigated multilayer thin-wall Inconel 625 parts and discovered that parameter changes to decrease the top surface roughness might increase the side wall roughness [124]. Moreover, it should be noted that unmelted powders cause higher surface roughness and crack initiation [125]. Experimental results reported in recent publications disclose the strong influence of surface roughness on HX efficiency [10, 21, 90]. Rough surfaces proved to provide higher heat transfer and higher pressure losses over a large range of Reynolds numbers [126, 127]. In practice, higher surface roughness may cause disruption of thermal boundary layer and early transition to turbulence [10, 92]. Ventola et al. produced artificial surface roughness in heat sink fins through SLM which resulted in 35% average increase in heat transfer [92]. The authors in [92] observed on average 80% better convective heat transfer enhancement on rough flat surfaces compared to rough finned surfaces. A detailed discussion on surface roughness characterization is presented in Section 4. The main challenge in metal AM is the near-net-shape fabrication. One can always expect a geometric difference between a printed object and the CAD known as stair-stepping effect [59]. This demands an essential post-processing which might be more expensive and more time


15
consuming than the printing process. In practice, the post processing is more challenging for inclined surfaces, internal surfaces of microchannels, pin fin arrays, thin cross-sectional areas, and support removal. Moreover, the necessity of support structures add complexity to the design process. It can also increase the total cycle time and the total fabrication cost. In effect, there are features which are left unsupported. Those features are difficult to access and will end up with rough surfaces. Some features, e.g. downward facing surfaces, can be oriented at a 45° angle to ensure partial support. Researchers have been working on overhang angles to reduce the need for support mainly as a two-dimensional problem [65, 128]. More research is needed in this regard to ensure manufacturability for three-dimensional fabrication [66]. To this end, an appropriate setting of a variety of process parameters is still a major challenge toward desirable AM product. The parameters include but not limited to powder characteristics, heat power, beam diameter, scanning speed, layer thickness, and hatch spacing. In practice, these parameters have direct effect on the build time as well as the duration of exposure to the heat source which has impact on microstructural properties and dimensional variances. Another important fabrication challenge is the cleaning of complex internal channels. For selective laser melting (SLM), powder entrapped or sintered in the channels and must be cleaned before further heat treatment or processing. If the channels have no opening or outlet, powder removal will be a challenge. For direct energy deposition (DED), the low heat flux and possibility to adjust the optics can control the distortion and minimize the problem of powder removal in the future.
4. Experimental evaluation of Additively Manufactured HXs
Although AM offers new design opportunities, it is still essential to characterize the HXs performance through experiments or in actual applications. Experimental characterization of thermal and hydrodynamic performance of HXs has been investigated by a handful of researchers, for instance [1], [3], [10], [47]. This section is about to highlight the significant insights in experimental evaluation of additively manufactured HXs. The important considerations in such experimental evaluations include, but are not limited to, characterizing flow structures and flow maldistribution, measuring pressure drop inside a manifold, determining air side pressure drop, scaling up microchannel heat exchangers, producing velocity profiles, determining sources of experimental uncertainty, testing over large range of Reynolds numbers, using conventional HX correlations for newly designed AM heat exchangers, comparing baseline and AM heat exchangers in various flow rates, flow structure visualization, analyzing Nusselt number and friction factor, characterizing the turbulence intensity and the turbulent kinetic energy for miniature geometries, e.g. in fin array [47], and analyzing the impact of turbulence quantities on thermal and hydrodynamic performance in compact HX design [47]. As discussed in earlier sections, AM leaves rough surfaces. Such surface roughness can affect the hydrodynamic and heat transfer performance of AM parts. Many studies have reported roughness-induced early transition from laminar flow to turbulent flow [47, 53, 90, 93, 96, 129, 130]. Stimpson et al. [90] measured air pressure drop through AM channels with design hydraulic diameters between 635 and 1261 μμm. Based on air flow pressure drop, they reported significant augmentation of friction factor in additively manufactured coupons compared to smooth channels.


