5. Summary and conclusions
An ideal HX design ensures balance among design specifications (i.e. size, weight, material), performance characteristics (i.e. heat transfer and pressure drop), and other requirements such as reliability and maintainability. The primary objective in HX design is enhancing heat transfer rate at a minimal pressure drop (improving energy efficiency and power density). AM offers tremendous advantages to the design and manufacturing of HXs including
• An ability to fabricate optimized geometries with enhanced surfaces. In essence, AM has provided the opportunity of exploring novel designs and overcoming the limitations of conventional fabrication methods.
• Consolidation and monolithic bonds which eliminates the need for weld or braze and may reduce the production cycle time. In essence, inappropriate welding or brazing may result in clogging (e.g. in microchannels). It is helpful in improving reliability particularly in high pressure applications. Furthermore, integrating HPs into the structure would be beneficial for novel energy storage systems.
• The opportunity for controlled surface roughness with the goal of heat transfer enhancement through innovative heat transfer surface designs. Designers of additive manufacturing parts must realize how additive manufacturing can affect surface roughness as it impacts the amount of cooling that can be achieved in actual applications. The existing research proves that upward-facing surfaces have smaller roughness than downward-facing surfaces. Further development is needed to explore the desirable surface roughness in the design phase and characterize the effects of increased surface roughness on thermal efficiency. In this respect, particular attention should be paid to adjoining internal surfaces of microchannels, e.g. pin fin arrays, which are not easily accessible.
• Unique opportunity for fully controlled and organized porous structures. Porous structures (including lattice structures) are favorable for conductive heat transfer improvement.
Despite various research efforts into applying AM technologies for HXs, it is clear that there are still many research opportunities that require further investigation:
• Materials are still the cornerstone of AM development and needs more fundamental research particularly for high temperature HX applications. In this respect, the literature


21
suggests investigating more aerospace-grade materials, ceramic material with twodimensional reinforcement, copper, certain Haynes alloys, and even composites. It is recognized that low temperature conventional heat exchanger designs are uneconomical in high temperature (>500°C) applications as they encounter creep, reduced strength, oxidation, and thermal shock.
• Traditional heat exchangers use the configuration of shell and tube, plate and fin, or plate and frame. AM offers unprecedented design freedom to fabricate HX with customized geometries. However, the so-called assertion of “complexity is free in AM” would not work well when it comes to internal features. The degree of complexity for internal feature is dependent on manufacturing process capability.
• Design for AM requires a particular thought process as well as thorough understanding of various AM technologies. The literature suggests innovative design-for-additive solutions for fabrication beneficial features and achieving desirable surface conditions especially for mini- and micro-channels. It is advantageous to combine topology optimization with AM methods for metal with higher thermal conductivity [51]. Design and manufacturing of complex and compact HX through AM can be easily justified if it can be extended to new and wide range of applications.
• Despite tremendous progress, fabrication inaccuracy and adequate reproducibility will remain a key issue for micro geometries.
• AM technologies and applications have experienced continuous advancements in the past decade. In near future, one can expect enhanced manufacturability, sophisticated simulation packages, and advanced process control tools for metal AM. In particular, accurate simulation is needed to quantify heat performance in multiphase HXs with porous media, capillary structures, and manifold-microchannel HXs.
• Thus far, SLM has been the most popular metal AM technology for HX applications mainly because of high-resolution and high flexibility. It seems SLM will keep its popular position particularly for innovative passive thermal systems. Nevertheless, enhanced SLM systems with less production time and cost would be very advantages for large scale production of additively manufactured HXs.
• Performance characterization will remain a challenge for a newly developed HXs particularly for complex two-phase devices. In this respect, more data is needed to evaluate the thermal performance of additively made HXs. In addition, it should be noted that in the new HX design trend minimizing pressure drop has not been considered as much as increasing the surface to volume ratio.
• New methods of reliability analysis are required for miniature geometries and thin walls which are being used in novel compact HX designs.
